// deeply nested expressions.
//
// C11 §5.2.4.1 obliges a conforming implementation to handle only 63
// nesting levels of parenthesised expressions within a full expression.
// That is a MINIMUM, and gcc and clang go far past it — but they go past
// it by recursing, so "far past" means "until the stack runs out", and the
// failure mode is a compiler segfault with no diagnostic, on the user's
// machine, in generated code they never wrote.
//
// a Zen source expression need not be deep for the emitted C to be: an
// inlined `.then`, a chain of `.match` arms, or a fold body pasted at the
// call site all multiply. So this file is not really about a user writing
// 256 parens; it is about establishing that gen_c may not assume its
// output nesting is bounded by its input nesting.
//
// the fix, when it goes red, is not a bigger stack. It is to flatten into
// statement-level temporaries — which also makes the emitted C readable,
// and readable emitted C is what makes the seed reviewable at all.
//
// 256 additions here, 300 bare parentheses on the second line: both past the
// C11 minimum by a wide margin and well inside what a real toolchain
// handles, so a red here is a gen_c bug and not a C compiler limit.

